# Handling HTTP Parsing Errors

## Handling 400 Bad Request Responses

Your approach for handling 400 Bad Request errors is sound. Using the StaticFileHandler to build error responses provides consistency in your error-handling flow.

```cpp
void WebServer::handleParsingError(Connection* conn) {
    // Create response object if it doesn't exist
    if (!conn->response_data_) {
        conn->response_data_ = new HttpResponse();
    }

    // Set up 400 Bad Request
    conn->response_data_->status_code = 400;
    conn->response_data_->status_message = "Bad Request";

    // Explicitly set Connection: close header
    conn->response_data_->headers["Connection"] = "close";

    // Let StaticFileHandler build the error page
    static_file_handler_.buildErrorResponse(conn, 400);

    // Update connection state for writing
    conn->state_ = Connection::CONN_WRITING;

    // Update epoll to watch for write readiness
    struct epoll_event ev;
    ev.events = EPOLLOUT;
    ev.data.fd = conn->client_fd_;
    epoll_ctl(epoll_fd_, EPOLL_CTL_MOD, conn->client_fd_, &ev);
}
```

## Virtual Servers and Bad Requests

When you receive a malformed request with multiple VirtualServers on the same port:

1. **Which server handles it?** Use the default server for that port:
   - If the Host header was parsed before the error occurred, you could use the matching VirtualServer
   - If no Host header was found (error occurred before it), use the default VirtualServer for the port

2. **HTTP compliance:** According to RFC 7230 (HTTP/1.1), servers SHOULD close connections after responding with 400 Bad Request

```cpp
void WebServer::parseRequest(Connection* conn) {
    // ...parsing logic...

    if (result == PARSE_ERROR) {
        // Find the right server to generate the error response
        VirtualServer* error_server = conn->virtual_server_;

        // If no virtual server was assigned yet (headers not fully parsed),
        // use the default server for this port
        if (!error_server) {
            int port = getConnectionPort(conn);
            error_server = default_servers_[port];
            // You might want to temporarily assign this server
            conn->virtual_server_ = error_server;
        }

        // Generate and send 400 response
        handleParsingError(conn);

        // Don't attempt to keep this connection alive
        // ResponseWriter will close it after sending
        return;
    }

    // ...continue with normal flow...
}
```

## Connection Handling After 400 Response

After sending a 400 Bad Request, you should **always close the connection**:

1. Modify ResponseWriter to check for this condition:

```cpp
bool ResponseWriter::isResponseComplete(Connection* conn) {
    if (conn->write_buffer_offset_ == conn->write_buffer_.size()) {
        // Response fully sent

        // Special handling for 400 Bad Request - always close
        if (conn->response_data_ && conn->response_data_->status_code == 400) {
            return false; // Don't attempt keep-alive
        }

        return true;
    }
    return false;
}
```

2. In your WebServer::handleWriteEvent method:

```cpp
void WebServer::handleWriteEvent(Connection* conn) {
    // ...write logic...

    if (response_writer_.isResponseComplete(conn)) {
        // For 400 responses, always close
        if (conn->response_data_ && conn->response_data_->status_code == 400) {
            connection_manager_.closeConnection(conn);
            return;
        }

        // For other responses, handle keep-alive normally
        if (conn->is_keep_alive()) {
            conn->reset_for_keep_alive();
            // Switch back to reading mode
            // ...
        } else {
            connection_manager_.closeConnection(conn);
        }
    }
}
