# Virtual Server Management in WebServer

Your approach is on the right track, but I'd recommend a slightly modified structure for clarity and correctness:

## Improved WebServer-to-Connection Design

```cpp
class WebServer {
private:
    // Map port numbers to vectors of VirtualServers listening on that port
    std::map<int, std::vector<VirtualServer*>> port_to_servers_map_;

    // Map port numbers to their default VirtualServer (first in config)
    std::map<int, VirtualServer*> port_to_default_server_;

    // Map listener socket FDs to their port number
    std::map<int, int> listener_fd_to_port_map_;

    // Map hostname to VirtualServer for quicker lookup
    std::map<std::string, VirtualServer*> hostname_to_server_;

    // Active connections by client FD
    std::map<int, Connection*> connections_;
};
```

## Connection Structure

```cpp
struct Connection {
    // ...existing fields...

    // Instead of storing the entire vector of servers:
    VirtualServer* virtual_server_;           // Current virtual server based on Host header
    VirtualServer* default_virtual_server_;   // Default server for this port (for reset)
    const ServerConfig* server_config_;       // Points to virtual_server_->getConfig()

    // ...other fields...
};
```

## Connection Constructor

```cpp
Connection::Connection(int fd, VirtualServer* default_server)
    : client_fd_(fd),
      virtual_server_(default_server),
      default_virtual_server_(default_server),
      server_config_(&default_server->getConfig()),
      // other initializations...
{
    // ...
}
```

## Benefits of This Approach

1. **Cleaner Separation**: Connection only stores what it needs - the current and default VirtualServer
2. **More Efficient**: No need to pass or store entire vectors in each Connection
3. **Better Encapsulation**: WebServer manages the mappings between ports, hostnames, and VirtualServers
4. **Clearer Reset Process**: When resetting a Connection, you simply set `virtual_server_ = default_virtual_server_`

## Flow Example

```cpp
// When accepting a new connection
int handleNewConnection(int listener_fd) {
    // Accept connection
    int client_fd = accept(listener_fd, ...);

    // Get port from listener socket
    int port = listener_fd_to_port_map_[listener_fd];

    // Get default server for this port
    VirtualServer* default_server = port_to_default_server_[port];

    // Create connection with default server
    Connection* conn = new Connection(client_fd, default_server);
    connections_[client_fd] = conn;

    // ...
}

// After parsing headers
void updateVirtualServer(Connection* conn) {
    std::string host = extractHostHeader(conn->request_data_);
    if (!host.empty()) {
        auto it = hostname_to_server_.find(host);
        if (it != hostname_to_server_.end()) {
            // Update to the appropriate virtual server
            conn->virtual_server_ = it->second;
            conn->server_config_ = &it->second->getConfig();
        }
    }
}
```

This structure is cleaner and more aligned with standard web server architecture for virtual hosting.

