# Hostname to IP Resolution in Socket Binding

Your current implementation with `inet_addr()` **does not resolve hostnames** like "localhost" to IP addresses like "127.0.0.1". It treats them as separate entities.

## The Issue

Looking at your socket binding code:

```cpp
if (host == "0.0.0.0") {
    addr.sin_addr.s_addr = INADDR_ANY;
} else {
    addr.sin_addr.s_addr = inet_addr(host.c_str());
}
```

The `inet_addr()` function only converts string IP addresses ("127.0.0.1") to binary form. It **cannot resolve hostnames** like "localhost". If you pass "localhost" to `inet_addr()`, it will return `INADDR_NONE` (-1), causing your bind to fail.

## Fix: Add Hostname Resolution

To support both hostnames and IP addresses, you should use `getaddrinfo()`:

```cpp
bool WebServer::setup_listener_sockets() {
    // Previous code...

    // Bind to the specified host:port
    struct sockaddr_in addr;
    memset(&addr, 0, sizeof(addr));
    addr.sin_family = AF_INET;
    addr.sin_port = htons(port);

    if (host == "0.0.0.0") {
        addr.sin_addr.s_addr = INADDR_ANY;
    } else {
        // Try to resolve hostname or use IP directly
        struct addrinfo hints, *res;
        memset(&hints, 0, sizeof(hints));
        hints.ai_family = AF_INET;
        hints.ai_socktype = SOCK_STREAM;

        int status = getaddrinfo(host.c_str(), NULL, &hints, &res);
        if (status != 0) {
            std::cerr << "Failed to resolve hostname: " << host 
                      << ", error: " << gai_strerror(status) << std::endl;
            close(listener_fd);
            return false;
        }

        // Use the resolved address
        addr.sin_addr = ((struct sockaddr_in*)res->ai_addr)->sin_addr;
        freeaddrinfo(res); // Don't forget to free
    }

    // Continue with bind() and listen()...
}
```

With this change, your code will properly handle both:
- IP addresses ("127.0.0.1", "192.168.1.100")
- Hostnames ("localhost", "example.com")

This ensures your virtual hosts will work correctly regardless of whether they're configured with hostnames or IP addresses.

