# Converting Range-Based Loop to C++98 Iterator Loop

Your current code uses a range-based for loop (C++11 feature):

```cpp
for (const std::string& server_name : server_ptr->server_names_) {
    hostname_to_virtual_server_[server_name] = server_ptr;
}
```

To make this compatible with C++98, you need to use explicit iterators:

```cpp
for (std::vector<std::string>::const_iterator it = server_ptr->server_names_.begin(); 
     it != server_ptr->server_names_.end(); 
     ++it) {
    hostname_to_virtual_server_[*it] = server_ptr;
}
```

This approach:

1. Creates a const_iterator pointing to the beginning of server_names_
2. Continues until it reaches the end
3. Increments the iterator after each iteration 
4. Uses the * operator to dereference the iterator and access the string value

This is the standard C++98 way to iterate through containers when you don't need the index.

