The correct way to print `unsigned char` depends on what you want to display:

**1. For printable characters (as a character):**
```cpp
unsigned char c = 'A';
printf("%c\n", c);
// or
std::cout << static_cast<char>(c) << std::endl;
```

**2. For numeric value (as a number):**
```cpp
unsigned char c = 65;
printf("%u\n", static_cast<unsigned int>(c));
// or
std::cout << static_cast<unsigned int>(c) << std::endl;
```

**3. For hexadecimal representation:**
```cpp
unsigned char c = 65;
printf("0x%02x\n", static_cast<unsigned int>(c));
// or
std::cout << "0x" << std::hex << std::setfill('0') << std::setw(2) 
          << static_cast<unsigned int>(c) << std::endl;
```

**For your specific error log:**

In your code, you're trying to print an `unsigned char` in an error message:

````cpp
// Current (problematic):
log(LOG_ERROR, "Invalid character '%c' in CGI header name: %s",
    c, header_name.c_str());
````

**The fix depends on what you want to show:**

**Option 1 - Show as character (if printable):**
````cpp
log(LOG_ERROR, "Invalid character '%c' (0x%02x) in CGI header name: %s",
    static_cast<char>(c), static_cast<unsigned int>(c), header_name.c_str());
````

**Option 2 - Show as numeric value:**
````cpp
log(LOG_ERROR, "Invalid character (ASCII %u) in CGI header name: %s",
    static_cast<unsigned int>(c), header_name.c_str());
````

**Option 3 - Show as hex (most common for debugging):**
````cpp
log(LOG_ERROR, "Invalid character 0x%02x in CGI header name: %s",
    static_cast<unsigned int>(c), header_name.c_str());
````

**Why the cast is needed:**
- `unsigned char` is promoted to `int` when passed to variadic functions like `printf`
- But `%c` expects the value to be in the valid character range
- `%u` and `%x` format specifiers work better with explicit `unsigned int` cast for clarity

**Recommended fix for your code:**
````cpp
log(LOG_ERROR, "Invalid character 0x%02x in CGI header name: %s",
    static_cast<unsigned int>(c), header_name.c_str());
````

This will show the hexadecimal value of the invalid character, which is most useful for debugging HTTP header parsing issues.

