**Yes, absolutely!** You can register different file descriptors with different epoll modes (level-triggered vs edge-triggered) using a single epoll instance. Each file descriptor can have its own individual `epoll_event` configuration.

## How to Mix LT and ET in One Epoll Instance

````cpp
// Example: Register client socket as Level-Triggered (default)
struct epoll_event client_event;
client_event.events = EPOLLIN;  // No EPOLLET flag = Level-Triggered
client_event.data.fd = client_fd;
epoll_ctl(epoll_fd, EPOLL_CTL_ADD, client_fd, &client_event);

// Register CGI pipe as Edge-Triggered
struct epoll_event cgi_event;
cgi_event.events = EPOLLIN | EPOLLET;  // EPOLLET flag = Edge-Triggered
cgi_event.data.fd = cgi_pipe_fd;
epoll_ctl(epoll_fd, EPOLL_CTL_ADD, cgi_pipe_fd, &cgi_event);
````

## Practical Implementation for Your CGI Issue

Here's how you can modify your

 to support both modes:

````cpp
// In WebServer.h, add an overload:
bool register_epoll_events(int fd, uint32_t events, bool edge_triggered = false);

// In WebServer.cpp:
bool WebServer::register_epoll_events(int fd, uint32_t events, bool edge_triggered) {
    struct epoll_event ev;
    ev.events = events;
    if (edge_triggered) {
        ev.events |= EPOLLET;  // Add edge-triggered flag
    }
    ev.data.fd = fd;

    if (epoll_ctl(epoll_fd_, EPOLL_CTL_ADD, fd, &ev) == -1) {
        log(LOG_ERROR, "Failed to register fd %d with epoll: %s", fd, strerror(errno));
        return false;
    }

    log(LOG_DEBUG, "Registered fd %d with epoll (%s mode)", 
        fd, edge_triggered ? "Edge-Triggered" : "Level-Triggered");
    return true;
}
````

## Modified CGI Setup

````cpp
