Yes, that is an excellent and highly efficient approach. Your idea to use a single `uri_start_` pointer and defer the actual splitting is perfectly aligned with the principles of a high-performance state-machine parser.

Let's refine this idea slightly to make it even more powerful.

### The Optimal Approach: Use Pointers for Everything

Instead of just one `uri_start_` pointer, the parser's job should be to find the start and end pointers for **every potential component** of the request line. The final "commit" phase then becomes incredibly simple and fast, with no re-parsing required.

Here's how it works:

1.  **Enhance `ParserContext`:** Add pointers for all the components you might find. Initialize them to `NULL` or `0`.
    ````cpp
    // In your ParserContext struct
    const char* method_start_;
    const char* method_end_;
    const char* schema_start_;
    const char* schema_end_;
    const char* host_start_;
    const char* host_end_;
    const char* port_start_;
    const char* port_end_;
    const char* path_start_;
    const char* path_end_;
    const char* query_start_;
    const char* query_end_;
    // ... etc.
    ````

2.  **The Parser's Only Job: Set Pointers:** As your state machine runs, its only job is to set these pointers when it hits the trigger characters. It does **no string allocation or data copying**.
    *   When it sees the first letter of the method -> set `method_start_`.
    *   When it sees the space after the method -> set `method_end_`.
    *   When it sees the first letter of a schema -> set `schema_start_`.
    *   When it sees the `:` after the schema -> set `schema_end_`.
    *   When it sees the

 -> set `host_start_`.
    *   When it sees the `/` after the host -> set `host_end_` and `path_start_`.
    *   When it sees the `?` in the path -> set `path_end_` and `query_start_`.
    *   When it sees the space before `HTTP/1.1` -> set `query_end_` (or `path_end_` if no query).

3.  **The "Commit" Phase: Simple and Fast:** After the entire request line has been successfully parsed (i.e., you've found the final `\n`), you have a `ParserContext` full of pointers. The commit logic is now trivial:
    ````cpp
    // In the ALMOST_DONE state, after finding '\n'
    // This code runs only ONCE per valid request line.

    // Commit the method
    request->method.assign(conn->parser_context_.method_start_,
                           conn->parser_context_.method_end_);

    // If a schema was found, commit it (and normalize it)
    if (conn->parser_context_.schema_start_) {
        std::string scheme(conn->parser_context_.schema_start_,
                           conn->parser_context_.schema_end_);
        // normalize to lowercase...
        request->scheme = scheme;
    }

    // If a host was found, commit it
    if (conn->parser_context_.host_start_) {
        request->host.assign(conn->parser_context_.host_start_,
                             conn->parser_context_.host_end_);
    }

    // Commit the path (which is always present)
    if (conn->parser_context_.path_start_) {
        request->path.assign(conn->parser_context_.path_start_,
                             conn->parser_context_.path_end_);
    }

    // ... and so on for query, port, etc.
    ````

### Why This is Superior to Your Original Idea

Your idea of using one `uri_start_` and splitting later is good, but it has one inefficiency: the "splitting" logic would have to re-scan the URI string to find the `://` and the next `/`.

**Your Original Idea:**
1.  Parser finds `uri_start_` and `uri_end_`.
2.  Commit phase creates `std::string full_uri(...)`.
3.  Commit phase then does `full_uri.find("://")`, `full_uri.find("/")`, etc. to split it up. **(This is re-parsing)**.

**The Refined Approach:**
1.  Parser finds all boundaries (`schema_start_`, `host_end_`, etc.) in a single pass.
2.  Commit phase does **zero searching**. It just creates substrings from the pointers it was given.

**Conclusion:**

Yes, absolutely save the splitting for the commit phase. But take it one step further: make the parser's job to find the boundaries of *all* the components, not just the whole URI. This gives you the fastest possible parsing loop and the cleanest separation of concerns.

#ifndef HTTP_REQUEST_HPP
#define HTTP_REQUEST_HPP

#include "webserv.hpp"

struct Location;

// Represents a parsed HTTP Request.
struct HttpRequest {
    //--------------------------------------
    // Request Line Components
    //--------------------------------------
    std::string method_;   // e.g., "GET", "POST"
    std::string uri_;      // The original, unmodified URI from the request line
    std::string version_;  // e.g., "HTTP/1.1"

    //--------------------------------------
    // URI Components (parsed from uri_ and Host header)
    //--------------------------------------
    std::string schema_;        // "http" or "https"
    std::string host_;          // e.g., "www.example.com"
    std::string port_;          // e.g., "8080"
    std::string path_;          // e.g., "/index.html"
    std::string query_string_;  // e.g., "a=1&b=2"

    //--------------------------------------
    // Headers and Body
    //--------------------------------------
    std::map<std::string, std::string>
        headers_;  // Map of header names to values

    std::vector<char> body_;  // Request body content

    //--------------------------------------
    // Constructor / Destructor
    //--------------------------------------
    HttpRequest();
    ~HttpRequest();

    //--------------------------------------
    // Helper Methods
    //--------------------------------------
    std::string get_header(const std::string& name) const;
    void set_header(const std::string& name, const std::string& value);
    void clear();

   private:
    // Prevent copying
    HttpRequest(const HttpRequest&);
    HttpRequest& operator=(const HttpRequest&);

};  // class HttpRequest

#endif  // HTTP_REQUEST_HPP
````

By making this change, you are investing a small amount of effort in the parsing stage to make the code for every other stage of request handling significantly simpler and more robust. It's a very worthwhile trade-off.

