    char hex2 = path[i + 2];
// ...
````

### 2. Missing Security Step: Path Normalization

This is the most important missing feature. Decoding is only the first part of making a URI safe to use. The second, critical part is **normalization**, which prevents path traversal attacks.

Your function correctly decodes `/a/b/%2e%2e/c` into `/a/b/../c`. However, you are not doing anything with the `../` sequence. If you take this decoded path and append it to your web root (e.g., `/var/www/html`), you would get `/var/www/html/a/b/../c`, which resolves to `/var/www/html/a/c`. An attacker can use this to escape the intended directory and access sensitive files.

**The Fix:**
You need a separate step after decoding to process and sanitize the path. This is the "Stage 2" validation we've discussed.

1.  **Decode:** Use your

 function (with the bounds-check fix).
2.  **Normalize:** Create a new function that takes the *decoded* path and resolves all `.` and `..` segments.

Here is a conceptual `normalize_path` function:
````cpp
std::string normalize_path(const std::string& decoded_path) {
    if (decoded_path.empty() || decoded_path[0] != '/') {
        return ""; // Invalid path
    }

    std::vector<std::string> segments;
    std::string current_segment;
    std::stringstream path_stream(decoded_path);

    // Split path by '/'
    while (std::getline(path_stream, current_segment, '/')) {
        if (current_segment.empty() || current_segment == ".") {
            // Ignore empty segments (from "//") or "." segments
            continue;
        }
        if (current_segment == "..") {
            // If not at the root, go up one level
            if (!segments.empty()) {
                segments.pop_back();
            }
        } else {
            // A normal segment
            segments.push_back(current_segment);
        }
    }

    // Reconstruct the canonical path
    std::string canonical_path = "/";
    if (!segments.empty()) {
        for (size_t i = 0; i < segments.size(); ++i) {
            canonical_path += segments[i] + (i < segments.size() - 1 ? "/" : "");
        }
    }
    return canonical_path;
}
````

