### Summary Table

| Member Type | If Constructor is Omitted... | Best Practice |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `std::string`, `std::vector`, `std::map` | **Safe.** Default constructor is called, creating an empty container. | Still good to have an explicit constructor for clarity and future-proofing. |
| `int`, `bool`, `double`, etc. | **Unsafe.** Member is uninitialized (contains garbage). | **Must** be initialized in a constructor. |
| `char*`, `int*`, etc. (Raw Pointers) | **Unsafe.** Member is uninitialized (points to garbage). | **Must** be initialized in a constructor (usually to `NULL` or `0`). |

**Conclusion:** You are correct that the standard containers will be safely initialized to an empty state on their own. However, you should **absolutely keep the explicit constructor**. It costs nothing, makes your code clearer, and, most importantly, prevents future bugs if non-class members are ever added to the struct.

