, I see one **major** issue that you must address with this new model.

**The Problem: Blocking File I/O**

Your current handler does this:
````cpp
// ...
int fd = open(...);
// ...
struct stat file_info;
fstat(fd, &file_info);
// ...
std::vector<char> file_content(file_info.st_size); // <-- ALLOCATES MEMORY FOR THE ENTIRE FILE
ssize_t bytes_read = read(fd, &file_content[0], file_info.st_size); // <-- BLOCKS THE ENTIRE SERVER
// ...
conn->response_data_->body_.assign(file_content.begin(), file_content.end());
````
This is a **scalability killer**. If a user requests a 1GB file, your server will allocate 1GB of RAM and will **block its entire event loop** while it reads that 1GB from the disk. It will be unable to serve any other requests during that time.

**The Solution: Asynchronous File Handling**

The new event-driven model allows you to solve this beautifully. The

