### The Code Structure

This makes your event handlers very clean and logical.

````cpp
// This is the function called when an event happens on the client socket
void WebServer::handle_client_socket_event(Connection* conn, uint32_t events) {
    // --- Phase 1: Always handle I/O readiness ---
    if (events & EPOLLIN) {
        // Always read from the socket to drain the kernel buffer.
        // This appends data to conn->read_buffer_.
        if (read_from_socket(conn) <= 0) {
            // Handle client disconnection
            close_connection(conn);
            return;
        }
    }

    if (events & EPOLLOUT) {
        // Always try to write if the socket is ready.
        // This drains data from conn->write_buffer_.
        if (write_to_socket(conn) == WRITE_ERROR) {
            // Handle fatal write error
            close_connection(conn);
            return;
        }
    }

    // --- Phase 2: Use state to decide on processing ---
    // We only try to parse if we are in the reading state.
    if (conn->conn_state_ == READING_REQUEST && !conn->read_buffer_.empty()) {
        // The parser will consume data from read_buffer_ and might change
        // the connection state to EXECUTING_HANDLER if a full request is parsed.
        request_parser_->parse(conn);
    }

    // If the state was WRITING_RESPONSE, we did the I/O in Phase 1,
    // but we do no processing here. We wait until the write is complete.
}
````

This architecture is robust. It never ignores I/O events, correctly handles pipelining, and uses the state machine to ensure requests are processed in the correct order. You are not confused; you have correctly identified the most important design pattern in your server.

