 object is about to be destroyed (from your `ConnectionManager`), its destructor **must** loop through any remaining `IOContext` pointers in its vector and perform the full cleanup routine on them.

````cpp
// In Connection.cpp

Connection::~Connection() {
    // This destructor is the ultimate safety net. It ensures all associated
    // file descriptors and contexts are cleaned up, no matter when the
    // connection is terminated.

    for (size_t i = 0; i < io_contexts_.size(); ++i) {
        IOContext* ctx = io_contexts_[i];

        // 1. Remove the file descriptor from epoll's interest set.
        // It's crucial to do this before closing the FD to avoid race conditions.
        // The epoll_fd would need to be accessible here.
        epoll_ctl(epoll_fd_, EPOLL_CTL_DEL, ctx->fd, NULL);

        // 2. Close the file descriptor.
        close(ctx->fd);

        // 3. Delete the IOContext object itself.
        delete ctx;
    }

    // The vector itself will be destroyed automatically, no need to clear().

    // Also free the request and response data objects
    delete request_data_;
    delete response_data_;
}
````

This two-level approach (proactive cleanup by handlers, reactive cleanup by the destructor) is what makes the system robust.

---

### 2. How Many Open `IOContexts` Can a Connection Have?

The number is **variable** and is determined by the needs of the active request handler. This is precisely why a dynamic `std::vector` is the correct data structure.

Here's a breakdown of the maximum number of contexts for typical scenarios:

| Request Type | Client Socket | Static File | CGI Read Pipe | CGI Write Pipe | **Total Contexts** |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Simple `GET` (no file/CGI) | ✅ | | | | **1** |
| `GET` for a static file | ✅ | ✅ | | | **2** |
| `GET` to a CGI script | ✅ | | ✅ | | **2** |
| `POST` to a CGI script | ✅ | | ✅ | ✅ | **3** |
| Future Proxy Handler | ✅ | | | | **2** (client + upstream) |

As you can see, the number of active I/O channels per connection is small but not constant. A fixed-member design would be both wasteful (allocating space for CGI pipes on every static file request) and inflexible. The dynamic vector approach correctly allocates resources only when they are needed, which is the cornerstone of a scalable server.

